WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:06.190
&gt;&gt; When I was looking up center

00:00:06.191 --> 00:00:08.860
for artificial
intelligence in society,

00:00:08.861 --> 00:00:10.000
one of the first thing
that struck

00:00:10.001 --> 00:00:12.464
me is that it's a group of

00:00:12.465 --> 00:00:14.575
department of computer science

00:00:14.576 --> 00:00:17.210
working with the department
of social work.

00:00:17.211 --> 00:00:19.600
In my knowledge, you can't
get computer science

00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:22.090
to work with computer
engineering and so

00:00:22.091 --> 00:00:23.440
this is really
interesting that there

00:00:23.441 --> 00:00:26.585
is an interdisciplinary effort

00:00:26.586 --> 00:00:30.220
in University of
Southern California that there's

00:00:30.221 --> 00:00:32.200
a vision to understand that

00:00:32.201 --> 00:00:34.955
this kind of work happens
when we work together.

00:00:34.956 --> 00:00:38.150
We have today three folks who

00:00:38.151 --> 00:00:41.240
will be presenting
Milind, Eric Rice,

00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:43.760
and Bistra Dilkina but we also

00:00:43.761 --> 00:00:46.700
have Jordan Davis and
Phebe Vayanos who are

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:52.325
also faculty at- Sze Suen and
Brian as well, I'm sorry.

00:00:52.326 --> 00:00:54.650
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you.
This is so exciting.

00:00:54.651 --> 00:00:56.480
We've been working on
trying to put this

00:00:56.481 --> 00:00:58.580
together in collaboration with

00:00:58.581 --> 00:01:01.310
Microsoft Research and it's

00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:02.750
absolutely fantastic that this

00:01:02.751 --> 00:01:04.520
has come together we may excited.

00:01:04.521 --> 00:01:09.570
My name is Milind Tambe
and along with me here as

00:01:09.571 --> 00:01:12.140
it was just mentioned we have

00:01:12.141 --> 00:01:13.935
Eric Rice and Bistra Bilkina

00:01:13.936 --> 00:01:15.765
who'll jointly give
this presentation.

00:01:15.766 --> 00:01:17.270
I should start my clock because

00:01:17.271 --> 00:01:19.560
I want to be done
in 10 minutes and

00:01:19.561 --> 00:01:21.770
then Eric and Bistra

00:01:21.771 --> 00:01:24.945
will takeover and they'll
speak for 10 minutes each.

00:01:24.946 --> 00:01:29.630
Phebe Vayanos, Sze, and
Jordan and Bryan Wilder

00:01:29.631 --> 00:01:31.790
are students so together this

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.270
represents bulk of
the center's faculty.

00:01:34.271 --> 00:01:38.270
So, just to very
quickly tell you about

00:01:38.271 --> 00:01:42.840
our center VR this USC Center
for AI in Society or CAIS,

00:01:42.841 --> 00:01:44.990
it's an interdisciplinary center

00:01:44.991 --> 00:01:47.745
that six key faculty are
all present here today.

00:01:47.746 --> 00:01:51.260
There's 24 PhD students
you only got one here but

00:01:51.261 --> 00:01:52.970
the remaining ones are back there

00:01:52.971 --> 00:01:55.420
and we'll talk to them later.

00:01:55.421 --> 00:01:58.400
Our focus is AI for
societal problems and

00:01:58.401 --> 00:02:01.420
we are very keen on
starting projects in India.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:03.740
To show our keenness
I wanted to show

00:02:03.741 --> 00:02:05.815
two slides. No politics.

00:02:05.816 --> 00:02:08.120
So, we were really thrilled to

00:02:08.121 --> 00:02:10.925
be in the audience when
honorable Prime Minister

00:02:10.926 --> 00:02:13.700
talked about AI for
societal benefits

00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:16.745
in Mumbai and so very inspiring.

00:02:16.746 --> 00:02:19.590
Then, this was in
February and then in

00:02:19.591 --> 00:02:22.430
June when honorable External
Affairs Minister held

00:02:22.431 --> 00:02:26.290
a roundtable meeting on
artificial intelligence and there

00:02:26.291 --> 00:02:28.130
too we were honored to be

00:02:28.131 --> 00:02:29.580
invited and that's me you can

00:02:29.581 --> 00:02:32.070
see hiding behind in the back.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.120
Again, we talked about how

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:37.470
AI could be used for
societal benefits.

00:02:37.471 --> 00:02:40.225
So, we're very keen on
starting projects here.

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:42.170
Just to give you
a flavour for the kinds

00:02:42.171 --> 00:02:44.270
of projects that we
have done at USC,

00:02:44.271 --> 00:02:45.650
our focus has been on

00:02:45.651 --> 00:02:47.985
three areas public safety
and security,

00:02:47.986 --> 00:02:49.805
public health and conservation

00:02:49.806 --> 00:02:51.890
and I'll give
a general overview and

00:02:51.891 --> 00:02:53.420
then Eric will talk
in depth about

00:02:53.421 --> 00:02:56.070
public health and Bistra
about conservation.

00:02:56.071 --> 00:02:59.689
So, the basic problem we
focus on is how to optimize

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.700
our limited intervention
resources using

00:03:01.701 --> 00:03:05.019
AI in order to solve
societal problems.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.670
So, let's take public health,

00:03:06.671 --> 00:03:07.790
Eric will discuss this in

00:03:07.791 --> 00:03:09.950
more depth but give
you a concrete example

00:03:09.951 --> 00:03:11.750
HIV AIDS is a big problem with

00:03:11.751 --> 00:03:14.010
the homeless population
in Los Angeles.

00:03:14.011 --> 00:03:15.680
How to spread information about

00:03:15.681 --> 00:03:17.140
HIV amongst the homeless youth.

00:03:17.141 --> 00:03:18.410
We can't talk to everybody,

00:03:18.411 --> 00:03:20.105
there're 6,000 homeless youth.

00:03:20.106 --> 00:03:21.664
So, we have to identify

00:03:21.665 --> 00:03:23.905
key peer leaders who
will spread information.

00:03:23.906 --> 00:03:26.030
We show that AI
techniques are far more

00:03:26.031 --> 00:03:28.190
effective in finding
this peer leaders to

00:03:28.191 --> 00:03:30.215
spread information compared to

00:03:30.216 --> 00:03:31.910
traditional methods
and this we've

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:33.770
done with pilot test
on the ground so

00:03:33.771 --> 00:03:35.740
these are always things
that we test out.

00:03:35.741 --> 00:03:37.265
But can we try this out for

00:03:37.266 --> 00:03:41.115
TB prevention and for other
kinds of health challenges?

00:03:41.116 --> 00:03:44.360
For conservation, we've been
working a lot in Uganda for

00:03:44.361 --> 00:03:45.410
the past several years for

00:03:45.411 --> 00:03:46.850
the Uganda Wildlife Authority

00:03:46.851 --> 00:03:48.350
to protect endangered wildlife.

00:03:48.351 --> 00:03:51.410
Here, with machine learning
we predict that poachers will

00:03:51.411 --> 00:03:52.820
attack with traps or snares and

00:03:52.821 --> 00:03:54.610
remove them before
they kill animals.

00:03:54.611 --> 00:03:56.430
We've done this for
past several years,

00:03:56.431 --> 00:03:57.970
a lot of snares
have been removed,

00:03:57.971 --> 00:03:59.970
animals hopefully
have been saved,

00:03:59.971 --> 00:04:03.045
and with drones getting
infrared video,

00:04:03.046 --> 00:04:05.330
detecting poachers and animals on

00:04:05.331 --> 00:04:07.400
the fly from these infrared video

00:04:07.401 --> 00:04:09.220
so that they can be stopped.

00:04:09.221 --> 00:04:11.890
Bistra will discuss this
in much more depth.

00:04:11.891 --> 00:04:14.120
We have also a lot of
interesting public safety

00:04:14.121 --> 00:04:15.760
and security counter terrorism.

00:04:15.761 --> 00:04:18.020
We've worked a lot with
government agencies,

00:04:18.021 --> 00:04:20.780
coast guard, the Transportation
Security Administration.

00:04:20.781 --> 00:04:22.570
Large number of
targets to protect,

00:04:22.571 --> 00:04:23.860
where and when to patrol,

00:04:23.861 --> 00:04:26.765
where and when to put security
checkpoints, and so forth.

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:28.390
This is not the focus
of this workshop,

00:04:28.391 --> 00:04:29.780
I'm not going to
talk about it but

00:04:29.781 --> 00:04:32.450
these the software that's
actually in use for

00:04:32.451 --> 00:04:33.860
over a decade by these

00:04:33.861 --> 00:04:35.480
agencies just to
tell you that we're

00:04:35.481 --> 00:04:37.160
very interested in
getting the software

00:04:37.161 --> 00:04:38.695
deployed and in use.

00:04:38.696 --> 00:04:40.640
This is now spreading
to other countries like

00:04:40.641 --> 00:04:43.305
Israel and Argentina
and so forth.

00:04:43.306 --> 00:04:47.270
So, I wanted to talk a little
bit about the fact when

00:04:47.271 --> 00:04:49.070
we solve problems we firmly

00:04:49.071 --> 00:04:51.145
believe in interdisciplinary
partnerships.

00:04:51.146 --> 00:04:52.790
So, to that end we've really

00:04:52.791 --> 00:04:54.740
worked with the Coast Guard
patrolling with them

00:04:54.741 --> 00:04:57.410
on their Coast Guard ports
in New York or patrolled in

00:04:57.411 --> 00:04:58.760
the forests of Malaysia with

00:04:58.761 --> 00:05:00.500
the wildlife
conservation agencies.

00:05:00.501 --> 00:05:02.330
Eric has spent
15 years working with

00:05:02.331 --> 00:05:06.120
homeless populations in
Los Angeles and so on.

00:05:06.121 --> 00:05:08.945
So, when we start solving
problems we always start with

00:05:08.946 --> 00:05:12.260
immersing ourselves in the
domain and trying to get data.

00:05:12.261 --> 00:05:13.550
This is not a situation where

00:05:13.551 --> 00:05:14.880
data is ready made available.

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:17.910
We are very interested
in trying to identify,

00:05:17.911 --> 00:05:19.605
get the right data.

00:05:19.606 --> 00:05:21.470
Following that of
course the next step

00:05:21.471 --> 00:05:23.430
is predictive analytics that is,

00:05:23.431 --> 00:05:25.640
given the data can we predict

00:05:25.641 --> 00:05:28.505
which cases may be high risk
which may be low-risk?

00:05:28.506 --> 00:05:31.250
Many AI systems stop
there just predicting

00:05:31.251 --> 00:05:33.470
high and low risk cases but we at

00:05:33.471 --> 00:05:35.895
our center are interested
in doing more.

00:05:35.896 --> 00:05:38.480
With limited intervention
resources can

00:05:38.481 --> 00:05:41.200
we go beyond and do
prescriptive analytics?

00:05:41.201 --> 00:05:43.700
That is where to take our
limited resources and

00:05:43.701 --> 00:05:46.415
place them so that we
get the highest benefit

00:05:46.416 --> 00:05:48.965
not just identify
high and low risk but

00:05:48.966 --> 00:05:50.600
which cases should we

00:05:50.601 --> 00:05:52.700
take on given our
limited resources.

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:54.440
Now many AI systems will stop

00:05:54.441 --> 00:05:56.900
there but our center
we really are very

00:05:56.901 --> 00:05:59.060
interested in going
one step beyond and

00:05:59.061 --> 00:06:02.390
testing this prescription in

00:06:02.391 --> 00:06:05.480
the field and understanding
where we have gone right and

00:06:05.481 --> 00:06:09.080
wrong so that this can then
inform our data collection.

00:06:09.081 --> 00:06:11.420
Very quickly just
as an example in

00:06:11.421 --> 00:06:12.750
the wildlife conservation domain

00:06:12.751 --> 00:06:14.125
data collection would be,

00:06:14.126 --> 00:06:16.040
where are the snares
place based on

00:06:16.041 --> 00:06:18.110
that predictive
analytics would say,

00:06:18.111 --> 00:06:20.240
where are the high-risk
areas in the park?

00:06:20.241 --> 00:06:22.105
Where are the low risk
areas in the park?

00:06:22.106 --> 00:06:24.170
Prescriptive analytics would say,

00:06:24.171 --> 00:06:25.700
where should you patrol given

00:06:25.701 --> 00:06:27.890
that you know high and
low risk areas?

00:06:27.891 --> 00:06:29.640
Finally testing in the field

00:06:29.641 --> 00:06:31.230
which I would send
out the patrollers,

00:06:31.231 --> 00:06:32.480
get them to patrol, do we

00:06:32.481 --> 00:06:34.210
get the results that
we want or not?

00:06:34.211 --> 00:06:37.365
If we don't then change
our model or algorithm.

00:06:37.366 --> 00:06:38.960
So, as I said all of

00:06:38.961 --> 00:06:41.170
the cases that I talked
about are deployed,

00:06:41.171 --> 00:06:42.620
tested we're very interested

00:06:42.621 --> 00:06:44.150
in actually testing things in

00:06:44.151 --> 00:06:45.560
the field and not

00:06:45.561 --> 00:06:48.050
just writing papers
and so this is very,

00:06:48.051 --> 00:06:50.160
very important to our center.

00:06:50.161 --> 00:06:52.370
I just wanted to talk about

00:06:52.371 --> 00:06:55.670
a few projects that we have in

00:06:55.671 --> 00:06:59.540
my last four minutes or so that

00:06:59.541 --> 00:07:01.100
may inform you about some of

00:07:01.101 --> 00:07:03.960
the ongoing efforts
at our center.

00:07:03.961 --> 00:07:06.625
Eric and Bistra will
talk more of course.

00:07:06.626 --> 00:07:08.090
So, for example AI can

00:07:08.091 --> 00:07:10.355
help in obesity
prevention efforts.

00:07:10.356 --> 00:07:12.830
This is an idea of
how could we optimize

00:07:12.831 --> 00:07:16.440
public outreach efforts
within limited budgets,

00:07:16.441 --> 00:07:18.080
can we help improve

00:07:18.081 --> 00:07:20.170
TB treatment by predicting
defaulters and I

00:07:20.171 --> 00:07:22.250
understand Amid may
have discussed some of

00:07:22.251 --> 00:07:24.845
the work that's going
on in this context.

00:07:24.846 --> 00:07:27.860
But let me take on this
childhood obesity prevention.

00:07:27.861 --> 00:07:29.790
So, this is in
the United States not

00:07:29.791 --> 00:07:33.220
a very large problem
childhood obesity.

00:07:33.221 --> 00:07:35.340
So, in Antelope Valley which is

00:07:35.341 --> 00:07:38.250
North of Los Angeles where
our university is located,

00:07:38.251 --> 00:07:40.130
there's a low-resource
community where

00:07:40.131 --> 00:07:41.420
only 10 percent of kids are

00:07:41.421 --> 00:07:43.355
perceived by their
parents to be healthy.

00:07:43.356 --> 00:07:45.920
So, we're working with
our school of medicine

00:07:45.921 --> 00:07:48.860
to try to see if we can
prevent childhood obesity.

00:07:48.861 --> 00:07:52.430
So, this is done because
the basic idea is trying

00:07:52.431 --> 00:07:54.140
to get peers to

00:07:54.141 --> 00:07:56.440
influence parents to get
children to eat healthy.

00:07:56.441 --> 00:07:58.490
In other words, get
mothers to cook together

00:07:58.491 --> 00:08:00.740
healthy food but
which mothers should cook

00:08:00.741 --> 00:08:02.720
together because that's the wave

00:08:02.721 --> 00:08:05.275
we'll determine health outcomes.

00:08:05.276 --> 00:08:07.190
So, essentially if
you look at it from

00:08:07.191 --> 00:08:08.480
a graph each of

00:08:08.481 --> 00:08:10.440
these nodes you could
imagine is a mother,

00:08:10.441 --> 00:08:13.700
the red node is the mother
whose children are unhealthy.

00:08:13.701 --> 00:08:16.750
So, who do we pair
this mother with in

00:08:16.751 --> 00:08:20.070
order to get the health
outcomes we want?

00:08:20.071 --> 00:08:21.905
So, should we get

00:08:21.906 --> 00:08:24.470
this one to connect with
this one and then maybe drop

00:08:24.471 --> 00:08:26.390
a connection to this other one
because that's not

00:08:26.391 --> 00:08:28.670
a healthy relationship of one

00:08:28.671 --> 00:08:31.190
this kind of a group
and so forth?

00:08:31.191 --> 00:08:33.400
So, those are the
questions we can solve.

00:08:33.401 --> 00:08:35.480
This is work that's going on in

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:36.920
Antelope Valley this is

00:08:36.921 --> 00:08:39.395
something that we're very
interested in deploying.

00:08:39.396 --> 00:08:42.845
Another project that's
also ongoing is worth

00:08:42.846 --> 00:08:46.340
when urban peak business in
Denver a homeless shelter.

00:08:46.341 --> 00:08:49.190
Here, we have a social
network of youth who

00:08:49.191 --> 00:08:52.459
are addicted, who
abuse substances.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:54.020
These are shown by the red nodes,

00:08:54.021 --> 00:08:55.355
the black nodes are ones who are

00:08:55.356 --> 00:08:56.990
healthy and the question then

00:08:56.991 --> 00:08:58.760
becomes can we create groups so

00:08:58.761 --> 00:09:01.080
that we reduce substance abuse?

00:09:01.081 --> 00:09:03.815
So, can you partition
the graph creating

00:09:03.816 --> 00:09:07.010
groups where
healthy outcome prevails

00:09:07.011 --> 00:09:08.300
because the peers can

00:09:08.301 --> 00:09:10.190
influence this red node
because they have been cut

00:09:10.191 --> 00:09:11.570
off from these other red node

00:09:11.571 --> 00:09:13.530
in order to change
their behaviour?

00:09:13.531 --> 00:09:15.600
So, these things
trying to figure out

00:09:15.601 --> 00:09:19.050
which partitions can
perform better so

00:09:19.051 --> 00:09:21.455
that we avoid deviancy
training because if you

00:09:21.456 --> 00:09:23.330
incorrectly create partitions

00:09:23.331 --> 00:09:24.970
then actually
addiction increases.

00:09:24.971 --> 00:09:26.420
So we want to reduce
my addiction by

00:09:26.421 --> 00:09:28.650
creating the right kinds
of partitions.

00:09:28.651 --> 00:09:32.810
A third challenge that we've

00:09:32.811 --> 00:09:34.690
recently taken on is

00:09:34.691 --> 00:09:37.075
trying to look at
infectious diseases.

00:09:37.076 --> 00:09:38.420
In India, we know there's

00:09:38.421 --> 00:09:40.790
half a million deaths due to TB.

00:09:40.791 --> 00:09:43.130
How to get more patients
into treatment.

00:09:43.131 --> 00:09:44.950
So, there's outreach effort,

00:09:44.951 --> 00:09:47.210
and some of you may know
a whole lot more about this,

00:09:47.211 --> 00:09:48.950
but this is what we
understand that there are

00:09:48.951 --> 00:09:51.020
different outreach efforts,
the question is,

00:09:51.021 --> 00:09:52.850
this different outreach efforts

00:09:52.851 --> 00:09:54.910
reach different segments
of the population,

00:09:54.911 --> 00:09:57.220
different geographical
areas, who to target,

00:09:57.221 --> 00:10:00.075
how to spread our limited budget.

00:10:00.076 --> 00:10:01.890
So, we've constructed

00:10:01.891 --> 00:10:03.840
a multi-agent model
for disease spread and

00:10:03.841 --> 00:10:06.230
an AI algorithm that based on

00:10:06.231 --> 00:10:08.060
realistic data can do

00:10:08.061 --> 00:10:10.290
an experimental evaluation
based on this.

00:10:10.291 --> 00:10:13.090
We show that our models can do

00:10:13.091 --> 00:10:16.005
better than what has
been done at status quo.

00:10:16.006 --> 00:10:19.260
Now clearly, this is done
as a simulation exercise

00:10:19.261 --> 00:10:22.430
based on realistic data
that we can get hold off,

00:10:22.431 --> 00:10:23.880
but the basic point is,

00:10:23.881 --> 00:10:25.235
if you have limited resources,

00:10:25.236 --> 00:10:28.280
how to invest them
in order to get

00:10:28.281 --> 00:10:30.065
the right kinds of outcomes

00:10:30.066 --> 00:10:31.780
based on whatever data we have.

00:10:31.781 --> 00:10:34.020
So, that's the type of
work that we can do.

00:10:34.021 --> 00:10:37.080
So, I'll end here
by saying there is

00:10:37.081 --> 00:10:39.125
a tremendous potential for use of

00:10:39.126 --> 00:10:41.685
AI for societal benefits.

00:10:41.686 --> 00:10:42.950
We are very interested in

00:10:42.951 --> 00:10:47.295
applications here of
your work Africa,

00:10:47.296 --> 00:10:49.600
in South America, and

00:10:49.601 --> 00:10:51.830
we're really keen on
pushing this forward.

00:10:51.831 --> 00:10:54.370
So, it's 10 minutes and I'm
going to hand this over

00:10:54.371 --> 00:10:57.740
to Eric to start his 10 minutes.

00:10:57.741 --> 00:11:00.415
&gt;&gt; I'm really excited
to be here as well.

00:11:00.416 --> 00:11:03.530
This is my opportunity to talk

00:11:03.531 --> 00:11:05.090
about two projects that are

00:11:05.091 --> 00:11:06.810
really near and dear to my heart,

00:11:06.811 --> 00:11:08.610
which are working with
homeless teenagers.

00:11:08.611 --> 00:11:10.950
So, I've been working with
homeless teenagers and

00:11:10.951 --> 00:11:14.485
young adults for the last
15 years in Los Angeles,

00:11:14.486 --> 00:11:17.080
and I'm going to talk
about one project that's

00:11:17.081 --> 00:11:20.735
on HIV prevention and another
project that's on housing.

00:11:20.736 --> 00:11:22.895
The point of this
is really to give,

00:11:22.896 --> 00:11:24.610
especially the folks who are in

00:11:24.611 --> 00:11:26.300
the NGO space in this room,

00:11:26.301 --> 00:11:28.650
a sense of how we approach
our work and also

00:11:28.651 --> 00:11:31.030
some of the kinds
of problems that

00:11:31.031 --> 00:11:33.350
we can tackle with technologies

00:11:33.351 --> 00:11:34.400
that maybe are things that you

00:11:34.401 --> 00:11:35.955
haven't thought about before.

00:11:35.956 --> 00:11:39.315
So, the first thing that
I want to mention and

00:11:39.316 --> 00:11:41.030
Jordan Davis here is with me as

00:11:41.031 --> 00:11:43.315
well from the School of
Social Work is that,

00:11:43.316 --> 00:11:46.765
social work researchers,
we're very invested in

00:11:46.766 --> 00:11:49.015
very intimate partnerships with

00:11:49.016 --> 00:11:50.520
non-government organizations

00:11:50.521 --> 00:11:51.740
and government organizations.

00:11:51.741 --> 00:11:53.090
So, Jordan and I have

00:11:53.091 --> 00:11:55.765
actually both done
intervention work,

00:11:55.766 --> 00:11:58.255
small-group work with,

00:11:58.256 --> 00:12:01.100
in his case youth

00:12:01.101 --> 00:12:03.200
who've been in substance
abuse treatment programs,

00:12:03.201 --> 00:12:06.860
in my case with
homeless teenagers and so,

00:12:06.861 --> 00:12:08.110
we don't just do research work,

00:12:08.111 --> 00:12:09.480
but we actually do work out in

00:12:09.481 --> 00:12:11.360
the world that
social service providers do.

00:12:11.361 --> 00:12:13.175
We do it hand-in-hand with them.

00:12:13.176 --> 00:12:15.060
So, this is actually
a picture of me actually

00:12:15.061 --> 00:12:17.210
interacting with a young person
out in the field.

00:12:17.211 --> 00:12:19.585
This is something that
happens on a regular basis.

00:12:19.586 --> 00:12:21.030
The issue is that,

00:12:21.031 --> 00:12:22.520
there are 4.2 million

00:12:22.521 --> 00:12:24.210
homeless youth in
the United States each year.

00:12:24.211 --> 00:12:27.820
There's 300 million citizens
of the United States.

00:12:27.821 --> 00:12:29.985
We're not nearly as
large as you all are,

00:12:29.986 --> 00:12:32.845
but homelessness
is a big problem.

00:12:32.846 --> 00:12:35.920
What this has suggested
recently is that

00:12:35.921 --> 00:12:39.760
almost one in 10 teenagers
between the ages of

00:12:39.761 --> 00:12:43.810
13 and 24 will experience
homelessness every year.

00:12:43.811 --> 00:12:46.470
So, this is becoming
an increasingly large problem

00:12:46.471 --> 00:12:50.130
as the class divide becomes
larger in the United States.

00:12:50.131 --> 00:12:53.050
HIV prevalence is
nearly 10 percent

00:12:53.051 --> 00:12:54.250
amongst this group of people.

00:12:54.251 --> 00:12:55.615
So, we really need
to think about,

00:12:55.616 --> 00:12:57.090
how can we target

00:12:57.091 --> 00:12:58.530
this very large group of people

00:12:58.531 --> 00:13:00.430
who need HIV prevention work?

00:13:00.431 --> 00:13:02.000
So, our community partners in

00:13:02.001 --> 00:13:04.350
this context are three different

00:13:04.351 --> 00:13:06.690
drop-in centers
where homeless youth

00:13:06.691 --> 00:13:08.500
can go and access services.

00:13:08.501 --> 00:13:10.070
One is Safe Place for Youth,

00:13:10.071 --> 00:13:11.110
another is My Friend's Place,

00:13:11.111 --> 00:13:13.325
another is the Los
Angeles LGBT center,

00:13:13.326 --> 00:13:14.695
where we've done this work.

00:13:14.696 --> 00:13:16.680
These are partner
organizations that

00:13:16.681 --> 00:13:19.510
I've been working with
for about 15 years.

00:13:19.511 --> 00:13:22.095
What we're trying to
do in this context

00:13:22.096 --> 00:13:24.205
is a peer-led intervention.

00:13:24.206 --> 00:13:25.420
So, the idea is,

00:13:25.421 --> 00:13:27.460
we don't have enough
resources to do

00:13:27.461 --> 00:13:29.060
HIV prevention work with

00:13:29.061 --> 00:13:31.210
every single homeless
youth in the city.

00:13:31.211 --> 00:13:35.410
But we can find strategic folks
who are the right people,

00:13:35.411 --> 00:13:37.295
who can be our advocates,

00:13:37.296 --> 00:13:39.140
who we can train to go and do

00:13:39.141 --> 00:13:41.695
health outreach work
in their community.

00:13:41.696 --> 00:13:43.360
The complexity of this,

00:13:43.361 --> 00:13:44.920
however is that
the social networks

00:13:44.921 --> 00:13:46.390
of these homeless
youth even within

00:13:46.391 --> 00:13:49.810
one of these centers are
these dizzying arrays.

00:13:49.811 --> 00:13:51.170
So, what we're stuck with is

00:13:51.171 --> 00:13:52.800
a problem that I
like to think of as

00:13:52.801 --> 00:13:55.960
very keen to the problem
that Google Maps solves.

00:13:55.961 --> 00:13:58.080
So, we're trying to
do optimal planning,

00:13:58.081 --> 00:13:59.570
and what we have is a network,

00:13:59.571 --> 00:14:00.975
where there's a lot of noise.

00:14:00.976 --> 00:14:02.375
There's a lot of uncertainty.

00:14:02.376 --> 00:14:05.110
We might not know exactly
who's connected to whom,

00:14:05.111 --> 00:14:06.910
people might get
into fights with one

00:14:06.911 --> 00:14:09.105
another and stop being
friends with one another.

00:14:09.106 --> 00:14:10.630
So, this is a moving target.

00:14:10.631 --> 00:14:12.995
Much like traffic in
a city is a moving target.

00:14:12.996 --> 00:14:14.720
But like with Google
maps where you

00:14:14.721 --> 00:14:16.640
want a specific solution,

00:14:16.641 --> 00:14:20.010
we want a specific solution
to who should we pick is

00:14:20.011 --> 00:14:21.590
this small group of
young people that we

00:14:21.591 --> 00:14:23.690
can train to do this activity.

00:14:23.691 --> 00:14:25.570
So, we developed algorithms that

00:14:25.571 --> 00:14:27.890
parse the community
into smaller sections,

00:14:27.891 --> 00:14:30.150
find specific key leaders.

00:14:30.151 --> 00:14:31.790
I'm going to spare
you all of the math,

00:14:31.791 --> 00:14:33.385
just show you pictures today,

00:14:33.386 --> 00:14:35.630
and what's exciting
about this is that,

00:14:35.631 --> 00:14:39.040
when we compare these systems to

00:14:39.041 --> 00:14:40.410
the standard ways in which

00:14:40.411 --> 00:14:42.825
public health and social work
have done this in the past,

00:14:42.826 --> 00:14:44.485
what we see here is that,

00:14:44.486 --> 00:14:47.560
the AI driven solutions far

00:14:47.561 --> 00:14:52.400
outperform the usual status quo
Public Health Solutions,

00:14:52.401 --> 00:14:54.900
and we're looking at, in
these networks we're seeing

00:14:54.901 --> 00:14:57.565
about 70 percent of
the target youth were getting

00:14:57.566 --> 00:14:59.915
messages about
HIV prevention versus

00:14:59.916 --> 00:15:03.240
only about 27 percent
in the standard group.

00:15:03.241 --> 00:15:05.195
Then, when we're thinking about

00:15:05.196 --> 00:15:07.370
people going out and
getting HIV testing,

00:15:07.371 --> 00:15:08.470
which is one of the important

00:15:08.471 --> 00:15:09.630
things you need to do
if you're going to

00:15:09.631 --> 00:15:12.225
link people into
antiretroviral drugs,

00:15:12.226 --> 00:15:14.035
is that we see again,

00:15:14.036 --> 00:15:15.945
a big difference here in terms of

00:15:15.946 --> 00:15:18.490
those who were reached
by our messaging.

00:15:18.491 --> 00:15:21.220
We had about almost
30 to 40 percent of

00:15:21.221 --> 00:15:22.400
those young people went and got

00:15:22.401 --> 00:15:23.880
an HIV test and so therefore,

00:15:23.881 --> 00:15:24.960
could start to get engaged in

00:15:24.961 --> 00:15:26.270
treatment if they needed it,

00:15:26.271 --> 00:15:28.070
versus the standard
public health,

00:15:28.071 --> 00:15:31.360
arm of this field
deployment where we

00:15:31.361 --> 00:15:34.770
actually had no new testers
in that space.

00:15:34.771 --> 00:15:37.460
So, we see this not as
a perfect solution,

00:15:37.461 --> 00:15:41.330
but when Milton was putting
out this pathway of

00:15:41.331 --> 00:15:45.710
going from collecting data
with community partners,

00:15:45.711 --> 00:15:47.140
then doing predictive analytics,

00:15:47.141 --> 00:15:48.410
and then doing
prescriptive analytics

00:15:48.411 --> 00:15:49.525
and then doing field tests,

00:15:49.526 --> 00:15:51.400
this is one example of a project

00:15:51.401 --> 00:15:53.510
that we've been invested
in for several years

00:15:53.511 --> 00:15:55.120
and we're actually
currently right

00:15:55.121 --> 00:15:57.830
now just about to finish
out a trial where

00:15:57.831 --> 00:16:00.620
we've enrolled nearly a
thousand homeless young people

00:16:00.621 --> 00:16:04.500
over the last year and
a half into this program.

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:06.860
One last thing that I'll mention

00:16:06.861 --> 00:16:07.950
is that what seems to be of

00:16:07.951 --> 00:16:10.710
great benefit of some of
these AI programs is that,

00:16:10.711 --> 00:16:12.445
in an age in which

00:16:12.446 --> 00:16:14.170
especially young people are

00:16:14.171 --> 00:16:16.315
very excited about technology,

00:16:16.316 --> 00:16:19.875
these are two young men who
were picked by our algorithms

00:16:19.876 --> 00:16:22.450
to become peer leaders and

00:16:22.451 --> 00:16:25.150
they felt very empowered
by this entire experience,

00:16:25.151 --> 00:16:27.530
and they actually started
to do much better,

00:16:27.531 --> 00:16:29.085
and actually both
of these young men

00:16:29.086 --> 00:16:30.900
ended up getting jobs and housing

00:16:30.901 --> 00:16:33.550
within the two years

00:16:33.551 --> 00:16:36.055
after this initial program
went forward.

00:16:36.056 --> 00:16:38.590
So, the other program that
I want to talk about is a

00:16:38.591 --> 00:16:41.110
little bit earlier
in its development.

00:16:41.111 --> 00:16:42.650
This is something that
I've been working very

00:16:42.651 --> 00:16:44.430
closely with Phebe Vayanos
who's not going to get

00:16:44.431 --> 00:16:46.140
a chance to stand up
and speak but I'm going

00:16:46.141 --> 00:16:48.480
to get to actually speak for her

00:16:48.481 --> 00:16:49.920
which is not entirely fair

00:16:49.921 --> 00:16:52.940
but here we are
working on housing.

00:16:52.941 --> 00:16:57.110
So, I've been working with
governmental organizations and

00:16:57.111 --> 00:16:59.660
non-governmental organizations
in the United States

00:16:59.661 --> 00:17:03.325
around housing issues for
the last five or six years.

00:17:03.326 --> 00:17:04.800
This has been something
that's a relatively

00:17:04.801 --> 00:17:06.445
more recent body of my work,

00:17:06.446 --> 00:17:08.525
but again the issue
is one of scarcity.

00:17:08.526 --> 00:17:10.810
So, we have this huge number
of young people who are on

00:17:10.811 --> 00:17:12.120
the streets and we don't have

00:17:12.121 --> 00:17:14.420
nearly enough resources
to give them.

00:17:14.421 --> 00:17:16.240
The United States is
this very wealthy country with

00:17:16.241 --> 00:17:18.640
a very broken social
welfare system.

00:17:18.641 --> 00:17:20.740
So, we are working with

00:17:20.741 --> 00:17:22.975
the Los Angeles Housing
Service Authority.

00:17:22.976 --> 00:17:25.490
This is the governmental
organization

00:17:25.491 --> 00:17:27.360
in Los Angeles that is

00:17:27.361 --> 00:17:30.040
both the city and
the county in which were

00:17:30.041 --> 00:17:31.790
located and they provide

00:17:31.791 --> 00:17:33.895
most of the housing resources
for homeless youth.

00:17:33.896 --> 00:17:35.010
So, they're our partner in

00:17:35.011 --> 00:17:37.260
this particular effort and what

00:17:37.261 --> 00:17:38.780
we're trying to do here is

00:17:38.781 --> 00:17:40.865
something that is
very much like Uber.

00:17:40.866 --> 00:17:42.275
So, what the idea is,

00:17:42.276 --> 00:17:44.630
is that we have a small number

00:17:44.631 --> 00:17:46.935
of houses that are
available at any one time,

00:17:46.936 --> 00:17:48.470
we've got a large
number of people

00:17:48.471 --> 00:17:49.675
that are waiting for housing.

00:17:49.676 --> 00:17:52.010
So, how do we match
just like Uber would

00:17:52.011 --> 00:17:54.890
match drivers to riders
through this traffic?

00:17:54.891 --> 00:17:57.200
How do we match the right people

00:17:57.201 --> 00:17:59.340
to the right housing resources,

00:17:59.341 --> 00:18:01.515
so that we can do two things?

00:18:01.516 --> 00:18:05.669
We can optimize the success
of those housing resources

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:07.690
because since there's
so few we don't want

00:18:07.691 --> 00:18:10.020
to waste any precious resource,

00:18:10.021 --> 00:18:11.990
but the other idea is
that we want to make

00:18:11.991 --> 00:18:14.640
sure that people
are treated fairly.

00:18:14.641 --> 00:18:16.980
So, there's a lot of
discussion right now in

00:18:16.981 --> 00:18:20.670
the ethical area of AI
around bias and algorithms,

00:18:20.671 --> 00:18:24.815
and that if you just train
systems that are built on

00:18:24.816 --> 00:18:28.370
former datasets that
may have a lot of

00:18:28.371 --> 00:18:29.700
implicit bias in them you will

00:18:29.701 --> 00:18:31.580
just reproduce these biases.

00:18:31.581 --> 00:18:33.910
So, what we really want
to do in this is we

00:18:33.911 --> 00:18:36.170
want to improve on two things,

00:18:36.171 --> 00:18:38.885
fairness as well as
the actual efficiency.

00:18:38.886 --> 00:18:41.050
So, the current system for

00:18:41.051 --> 00:18:43.945
housing allocation
is very simplistic.

00:18:43.946 --> 00:18:47.420
What you get is, young people are

00:18:47.421 --> 00:18:49.190
asked about 50 questions

00:18:49.191 --> 00:18:50.750
and from these 50
questions they're

00:18:50.751 --> 00:18:54.540
assigned a score that ranges
from one to 17 and depending

00:18:54.541 --> 00:18:56.275
on whether they score eight

00:18:56.276 --> 00:18:58.330
or higher they get
really good services,

00:18:58.331 --> 00:19:01.460
if they score four to seven
they get rental subsidies,

00:19:01.461 --> 00:19:04.635
and if they score less than
four they get nothing.

00:19:04.636 --> 00:19:07.530
We've approached
this in the way that

00:19:07.531 --> 00:19:08.780
Mill described and I guess

00:19:08.781 --> 00:19:09.870
this is not showing up so well.

00:19:09.871 --> 00:19:11.030
So, this says machine-learning

00:19:11.031 --> 00:19:12.250
about prediction and then this is

00:19:12.251 --> 00:19:15.870
going to be policies
that are prescriptive.

00:19:15.871 --> 00:19:17.730
But what we really
want to do with

00:19:17.731 --> 00:19:19.190
these policies is we want to

00:19:19.191 --> 00:19:20.960
make sure that they're
interpretable, right?

00:19:20.961 --> 00:19:23.300
This simplistic
scoring mechanism,

00:19:23.301 --> 00:19:24.780
although it's maybe not

00:19:24.781 --> 00:19:26.080
the most effective thing
in the world

00:19:26.081 --> 00:19:27.595
which I'll show you in a minute.

00:19:27.596 --> 00:19:30.120
It least is something that
people can understand.

00:19:30.121 --> 00:19:32.345
So, we want to make sure
that the AI that we build

00:19:32.346 --> 00:19:34.800
in these social
service contexts is

00:19:34.801 --> 00:19:36.810
one that where people can

00:19:36.811 --> 00:19:40.025
explain to their clients
that they're working with.

00:19:40.026 --> 00:19:43.050
Why they are or are not
eligible for services?

00:19:43.051 --> 00:19:44.960
We can't just deny
people housing and

00:19:44.961 --> 00:19:47.565
say well the computer says
you don't get housing.

00:19:47.566 --> 00:19:49.490
So, what we're interested in is

00:19:49.491 --> 00:19:51.195
optimizing our success like

00:19:51.196 --> 00:19:52.740
Uber but we also want to make

00:19:52.741 --> 00:19:54.825
sure that we avoid
discrimination.

00:19:54.826 --> 00:19:56.975
One of the big issues
in the United States

00:19:56.976 --> 00:19:58.625
is racial discrimination.

00:19:58.626 --> 00:20:01.200
So, these are
Hispanic and black youth,

00:20:01.201 --> 00:20:04.320
these are traditionally
populations that have suffered

00:20:04.321 --> 00:20:06.020
a lot of discrimination and

00:20:06.021 --> 00:20:08.070
marginalization in
the United States.

00:20:08.071 --> 00:20:10.080
This I want you to
just only focus

00:20:10.081 --> 00:20:11.980
in on the top and
the bottom here.

00:20:11.981 --> 00:20:13.500
That I don't have enough time
to explain everything in

00:20:13.501 --> 00:20:15.560
between but the top
is the current system

00:20:15.561 --> 00:20:17.280
as it exists and what you

00:20:17.281 --> 00:20:19.375
see here is that the white
youth that are purple,

00:20:19.376 --> 00:20:21.350
they're benefiting
at much higher rates

00:20:21.351 --> 00:20:23.265
than the Hispanic
and the black youth.

00:20:23.266 --> 00:20:27.200
Freebies algorithm is
able to change this.

00:20:27.201 --> 00:20:29.060
So, this is a
computational experiment

00:20:29.061 --> 00:20:31.210
where we're doing
some prescriptive analytics

00:20:31.211 --> 00:20:32.990
and we're saying
if we changed who

00:20:32.991 --> 00:20:35.330
we gave the housing
interventions to,

00:20:35.331 --> 00:20:37.470
so we didn't just do
this simplistic system

00:20:37.471 --> 00:20:38.895
that is currently in place.

00:20:38.896 --> 00:20:41.240
We can greatly reduce by

00:20:41.241 --> 00:20:42.380
looking at the individual

00:20:42.381 --> 00:20:44.095
characteristics of
these young people,

00:20:44.096 --> 00:20:45.320
we could greatly reduce

00:20:45.321 --> 00:20:47.890
the inequalities here and
what's even more exciting

00:20:47.891 --> 00:20:49.100
again focus on the top and

00:20:49.101 --> 00:20:51.260
the bottom is that
you can see this is

00:20:51.261 --> 00:20:53.840
the overall successful number

00:20:53.841 --> 00:20:55.780
of outcomes in
the system as a whole,

00:20:55.781 --> 00:20:58.160
and what we're able to do
is we're able to create

00:20:58.161 --> 00:21:01.015
a bump of about 10 percent
in this system,

00:21:01.016 --> 00:21:02.690
which is actually
a really big deal.

00:21:02.691 --> 00:21:04.000
This ends up being thousands of

00:21:04.001 --> 00:21:05.680
people when you're talking
about the scale of

00:21:05.681 --> 00:21:07.875
homelessness that you have in
Los Angeles where we've got

00:21:07.876 --> 00:21:11.060
50,000 people on the streets
on any given night.

00:21:11.061 --> 00:21:14.450
So with that, I will turn
things over to Bistra

00:21:14.451 --> 00:21:16.050
who's also going to talk
to you about some of

00:21:16.051 --> 00:21:18.420
the implications that
we've had recently.

00:21:18.421 --> 00:21:21.350
&gt;&gt; CAIS also had a lot
of projects that

00:21:21.351 --> 00:21:24.780
involve using AI for
wildlife conservation.

00:21:24.781 --> 00:21:26.940
As you all know there's been

00:21:26.941 --> 00:21:30.465
a dramatic decline in
the populations of many species,

00:21:30.466 --> 00:21:33.670
many of them in dangers
like tigers and elephants,

00:21:33.671 --> 00:21:38.005
as well as rhinos
and and giraffes.

00:21:38.006 --> 00:21:41.220
Unfortunately, a lot of
this decline is also

00:21:41.221 --> 00:21:44.675
due to poaching for
illegal wildlife trade.

00:21:44.676 --> 00:21:47.940
So, CAIS over the last four
to five years has actually

00:21:47.941 --> 00:21:51.335
partnered with several
NGO's such as WCS,

00:21:51.336 --> 00:21:54.755
WWF and Panthera to try
to help them to develop

00:21:54.756 --> 00:21:56.885
AI based tools to

00:21:56.886 --> 00:21:59.875
make the anti-poaching efforts
more effective.

00:21:59.876 --> 00:22:02.445
In the process we have
developed a tool called

00:22:02.446 --> 00:22:04.610
PAWS that helps them

00:22:04.611 --> 00:22:07.230
deploy patrolling efforts in

00:22:07.231 --> 00:22:10.525
protected areas to catch
poacher in the act.

00:22:10.526 --> 00:22:12.980
In fact, PAWS as I will show you

00:22:12.981 --> 00:22:15.390
later has become
so successful that

00:22:15.391 --> 00:22:17.460
now in the last
five months we have

00:22:17.461 --> 00:22:19.970
partnered with SMART
which is a consortium

00:22:19.971 --> 00:22:23.460
which includes this three
organizations as well

00:22:23.461 --> 00:22:27.760
as many more and SMART has a tool

00:22:27.761 --> 00:22:30.145
which helps many protected areas

00:22:30.146 --> 00:22:33.485
record their data in terms
of patrolling effort

00:22:33.486 --> 00:22:35.910
and detect illegal activity

00:22:35.911 --> 00:22:39.540
and build reports for
those protected areas.

00:22:39.541 --> 00:22:42.190
Now SMART is trying to

00:22:42.191 --> 00:22:45.510
incorporate our AI based tools
to actually provide them

00:22:45.511 --> 00:22:50.860
analytics and planning component
that will let them have

00:22:50.861 --> 00:22:53.830
more effective deployment in

00:22:53.831 --> 00:22:55.960
the field and therefore
we will allow us to

00:22:55.961 --> 00:22:58.340
reach more than 600 parks
in which SMART is

00:22:58.341 --> 00:23:01.705
currently used over
more than 55 countries.

00:23:01.706 --> 00:23:04.410
So, the way that PAWS works

00:23:04.411 --> 00:23:07.145
is actually again as
Mill mentioned earlier,

00:23:07.146 --> 00:23:10.250
starting from raw data
collected over

00:23:10.251 --> 00:23:12.870
the years in this parks
that includes

00:23:12.871 --> 00:23:14.510
the patrolling as well as

00:23:14.511 --> 00:23:16.290
the detections of
illegal activity,

00:23:16.291 --> 00:23:20.400
combining it with additional
information such as GS,

00:23:20.401 --> 00:23:22.470
information across
the park, on the roads,

00:23:22.471 --> 00:23:24.370
rivers, the park boundaries,

00:23:24.371 --> 00:23:25.700
land cover et cetera.

00:23:25.701 --> 00:23:29.460
And using that data to
build a predictive model of

00:23:29.461 --> 00:23:33.095
where we expect there
to be the highest risk

00:23:33.096 --> 00:23:37.250
of poaching given
the particular time

00:23:37.251 --> 00:23:39.540
of the year and
the particular characteristics

00:23:39.541 --> 00:23:41.600
of a grid cell in the park.

00:23:41.601 --> 00:23:45.045
Okay? And together with
this predictive model,

00:23:45.046 --> 00:23:47.990
then we are actually able
to help them with also with

00:23:47.991 --> 00:23:49.820
the prescriptive part saying with

00:23:49.821 --> 00:23:52.275
the limited resources of
park rangers they have,

00:23:52.276 --> 00:23:56.730
where should they send them
at any particular time.

00:23:56.731 --> 00:23:59.640
So, this is combining
things like machine

00:23:59.641 --> 00:24:03.220
learning together with
optimization and game theory.

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:05.900
So, as I mentioned,

00:24:05.901 --> 00:24:09.450
we have worked on this over
an extended period of time.

00:24:09.451 --> 00:24:10.860
In particular, we have worked

00:24:10.861 --> 00:24:13.065
closely with two parks in Uganda,

00:24:13.066 --> 00:24:15.845
Queen Elizabeth and
Murchison Falls.

00:24:15.846 --> 00:24:19.325
So, using historic data
from those two parks,

00:24:19.326 --> 00:24:21.550
we have developed
the progressively more

00:24:21.551 --> 00:24:23.910
and more accurate
machine learning models

00:24:23.911 --> 00:24:25.940
for the poaching risk,

00:24:25.941 --> 00:24:30.875
achieving accuracy of 0.86
in our best current model.

00:24:30.876 --> 00:24:32.535
But we don't stop there.

00:24:32.536 --> 00:24:36.395
Okay. That's
our accuracy in the lab.

00:24:36.396 --> 00:24:38.610
The most important thing
is to actually see whether

00:24:38.611 --> 00:24:41.500
these models really
work in the field.

00:24:41.501 --> 00:24:43.620
So, we went a step further,

00:24:43.621 --> 00:24:45.700
and actually had
fueled best when both

00:24:45.701 --> 00:24:48.560
parks over more between
six and nine months

00:24:48.561 --> 00:24:52.070
where we generated
patrolled areas

00:24:52.071 --> 00:24:54.530
where they had not
previously patrolled.

00:24:54.531 --> 00:24:57.000
Some of which areas
were predicted to be

00:24:57.001 --> 00:25:00.030
high risk and some low risk
based on our models,

00:25:00.031 --> 00:25:02.470
and later in Murchison Falls
refined that to

00:25:02.471 --> 00:25:04.300
even high medium and

00:25:04.301 --> 00:25:06.800
low risk without
telling the rangers,

00:25:06.801 --> 00:25:09.890
which is which they patrol
from six to nine months,

00:25:09.891 --> 00:25:11.260
and at the end of
the day, we just looked

00:25:11.261 --> 00:25:12.865
at the data of what happened.

00:25:12.866 --> 00:25:14.540
What we discovered that in fact,

00:25:14.541 --> 00:25:18.520
the areas where our model
was predicting high risk,

00:25:18.521 --> 00:25:19.945
they were able to find

00:25:19.946 --> 00:25:23.575
many more snares per patrol
effort that is spent there.

00:25:23.576 --> 00:25:27.725
So, in fact, it was extremely
accurate in allocating

00:25:27.726 --> 00:25:34.130
resources more effectively
than just guessing at random.

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:37.770
So, with this
predictive modeling,

00:25:37.771 --> 00:25:39.550
we can now that we
also know it works in

00:25:39.551 --> 00:25:41.910
the field were also
able to tell them more

00:25:41.911 --> 00:25:45.450
strategically of how exactly
to send their ranges to

00:25:45.451 --> 00:25:47.745
patrol and incorporate important

00:25:47.746 --> 00:25:49.019
on the ground constraints,

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:52.515
such as where other ranges
where should they start from,

00:25:52.516 --> 00:25:56.550
how much can they walk at
most in any given day, right?

00:25:56.551 --> 00:25:59.440
So, with the predictive model
and in this constraint,

00:25:59.441 --> 00:26:01.830
our optimization
game theoretic model

00:26:01.831 --> 00:26:04.100
is able to generate
maps like that,

00:26:04.101 --> 00:26:07.300
which says exactly where to
walk and how much effort to

00:26:07.301 --> 00:26:08.875
spend in each grid cell

00:26:08.876 --> 00:26:11.295
depending on the
characteristics of that,

00:26:11.296 --> 00:26:14.990
and the corresponding
prediction of

00:26:14.991 --> 00:26:19.550
how likely they are to detect
approaching activity there.

00:26:19.551 --> 00:26:24.105
So, we're really
excited about in fact,

00:26:24.106 --> 00:26:25.930
going to the deployment stage,

00:26:25.931 --> 00:26:27.490
working with smart,
and really getting

00:26:27.491 --> 00:26:29.810
the system out in the world.

00:26:29.811 --> 00:26:32.345
In addition to rangers,

00:26:32.346 --> 00:26:34.190
one additional resource that

00:26:34.191 --> 00:26:36.700
more and more protected areas
are thinking about is

00:26:36.701 --> 00:26:39.475
the use of UAVs in order to

00:26:39.476 --> 00:26:42.810
augment the patrolling of humans,

00:26:42.811 --> 00:26:44.290
NGO with which they're working on

00:26:44.291 --> 00:26:46.675
this, Cold Air Shepherd,

00:26:46.676 --> 00:26:51.405
which is using UAVs driven
in advance during the night,

00:26:51.406 --> 00:26:53.470
and sending those drones to

00:26:53.471 --> 00:26:56.930
actually patrol in
real-time for poachers,

00:26:56.931 --> 00:26:58.540
and having people sit,

00:26:58.541 --> 00:27:00.710
and trying to look
at the videos in

00:27:00.711 --> 00:27:02.760
real-time to detect
poachers called

00:27:02.761 --> 00:27:04.810
rangers if something is

00:27:04.811 --> 00:27:08.265
suspicious, and
respond effectively.

00:27:08.266 --> 00:27:11.270
Unfortunately, looking
at these videos is

00:27:11.271 --> 00:27:14.390
extremely difficult and tiring,

00:27:14.391 --> 00:27:19.300
and so their current setup
is not really scalable.

00:27:19.301 --> 00:27:21.300
So, they came to us and trying to

00:27:21.301 --> 00:27:24.010
help them to build
a more effective system.

00:27:24.011 --> 00:27:25.200
So, as you can see,

00:27:25.201 --> 00:27:27.730
this black and white videos
during the night with

00:27:27.731 --> 00:27:31.905
an infrared camera are extremely
difficult to interpret.

00:27:31.906 --> 00:27:33.610
They are very low-resolution,

00:27:33.611 --> 00:27:36.360
the objects we are trying to
detect that extremely small.

00:27:36.361 --> 00:27:37.805
The camera is moving,

00:27:37.806 --> 00:27:39.000
there's other things like

00:27:39.001 --> 00:27:40.560
vegetation and
reflection that look

00:27:40.561 --> 00:27:42.180
exactly like
the little specks that

00:27:42.181 --> 00:27:43.970
might be poachers, right?

00:27:43.971 --> 00:27:46.575
So, it's extremely hard problem.

00:27:46.576 --> 00:27:50.550
So, we built a system are
based on deep learning,

00:27:50.551 --> 00:27:54.760
which somehow the slides
are messed up.

00:27:54.761 --> 00:28:01.190
A system based on
deep learning to predict,

00:28:01.191 --> 00:28:02.895
to interpret these videos.

00:28:02.896 --> 00:28:06.500
Unfortunately, labeling
the videos is extremely time

00:28:06.501 --> 00:28:07.740
consuming and so we

00:28:07.741 --> 00:28:10.810
have a limited amount
of training data,

00:28:10.811 --> 00:28:14.240
but we partnered with
Microsoft in there

00:28:14.241 --> 00:28:17.300
video engine called
AirSim to actually

00:28:17.301 --> 00:28:19.720
create a version of it,

00:28:19.721 --> 00:28:21.990
which simulates
the African savanna,

00:28:21.991 --> 00:28:24.570
and poachers, and animals
in that African savanna.

00:28:24.571 --> 00:28:26.870
So, we are able to gather with

00:28:26.871 --> 00:28:28.940
also a model of a drone flying in

00:28:28.941 --> 00:28:31.415
the simulation to generate

00:28:31.416 --> 00:28:33.085
additional simulation data that

00:28:33.086 --> 00:28:34.980
looks very much
like the real data.

00:28:34.981 --> 00:28:37.340
So, together with the labeled

00:28:37.341 --> 00:28:40.230
very non-cost effective labeled,

00:28:40.231 --> 00:28:42.910
the real world data with
augmented simulated data,

00:28:42.911 --> 00:28:44.390
we are able to, no,

00:28:44.391 --> 00:28:45.770
I guess it's not going to show,

00:28:45.771 --> 00:28:48.140
the result on the side.

00:28:48.141 --> 00:28:52.290
To improve the accuracy
of our model,

00:28:52.291 --> 00:28:54.680
both in the recall
and precision much

00:28:54.681 --> 00:28:56.100
beyond their current system

00:28:56.101 --> 00:28:57.755
that they're using in the field.

00:28:57.756 --> 00:29:00.710
So, they're actually
currently testing it,

00:29:00.711 --> 00:29:04.520
using it in their actual events,

00:29:04.521 --> 00:29:09.580
and we also have
a Cloud-based implementation

00:29:09.581 --> 00:29:11.285
of the system.

00:29:11.286 --> 00:29:13.320
So, beyond anti-poaching efforts,

00:29:13.321 --> 00:29:16.330
we also have several projects

00:29:16.331 --> 00:29:18.140
related to conservation planning,

00:29:18.141 --> 00:29:19.770
can informing decision-making,

00:29:19.771 --> 00:29:22.390
makers in how to most
effectively uses

00:29:22.391 --> 00:29:26.060
very limited dollars allocated to

00:29:26.061 --> 00:29:31.005
conservation to generate
the best ecological impact.

00:29:31.006 --> 00:29:36.640
So, one such problem
we know how to address

00:29:36.641 --> 00:29:39.205
is how to inform

00:29:39.206 --> 00:29:42.680
preserving landscape
connectivity for species.

00:29:42.681 --> 00:29:44.880
So, as the habitat
gets more and more

00:29:44.881 --> 00:29:46.605
fragmented because of roads,

00:29:46.606 --> 00:29:48.850
urbanization, agriculture, etc.,

00:29:48.851 --> 00:29:51.200
essentially species
cannot move from

00:29:51.201 --> 00:29:54.095
one remnant of
habitat to another,

00:29:54.096 --> 00:29:58.725
which really jeopardizes
their long-term persistence.

00:29:58.726 --> 00:30:00.900
So, what we can do about this,

00:30:00.901 --> 00:30:03.345
we can try to selectively,

00:30:03.346 --> 00:30:07.470
try to intervene by for
example, building overpasses,

00:30:07.471 --> 00:30:09.540
over route or underpasses or

00:30:09.541 --> 00:30:12.515
protecting specific areas
from urbanization,

00:30:12.516 --> 00:30:14.315
and essentially providing

00:30:14.316 --> 00:30:16.445
wildlife corridors
for these animals

00:30:16.446 --> 00:30:20.890
to move and preserve
their populations.

00:30:20.891 --> 00:30:23.800
However, this is a very
difficult problem where you have

00:30:23.801 --> 00:30:25.430
multiple opportunities with

00:30:25.431 --> 00:30:27.770
different effects
and different costs,

00:30:27.771 --> 00:30:30.910
which interact with each
other because essentially,

00:30:30.911 --> 00:30:33.345
the combination of
all these interventions

00:30:33.346 --> 00:30:34.585
changed the landscape

00:30:34.586 --> 00:30:38.815
in terms of the best movement
pathways for these animals.

00:30:38.816 --> 00:30:41.760
So, we've developed
an algorithm that allows for

00:30:41.761 --> 00:30:45.120
us to produce such
intervention optimum plants.

00:30:45.121 --> 00:30:46.735
So, we've tested this in

00:30:46.736 --> 00:30:50.230
Western Montana where
a threatened species

00:30:50.231 --> 00:30:51.380
called the wolverine has

00:30:51.381 --> 00:30:55.210
several remaining populations
in the green parks,

00:30:55.211 --> 00:30:56.400
but which are basically

00:30:56.401 --> 00:30:59.400
disconnected because of
our high urbanization in

00:30:59.401 --> 00:31:01.480
the remaining carriers and

00:31:01.481 --> 00:31:03.520
the cost of intervention
is also very

00:31:03.521 --> 00:31:08.150
expensive in across
most of the landscape.

00:31:08.151 --> 00:31:09.760
So, with our method,

00:31:09.761 --> 00:31:12.100
we are able to
generate a subset of

00:31:12.101 --> 00:31:14.015
specific intervention
points here in

00:31:14.016 --> 00:31:16.265
red, which essentially reroute,

00:31:16.266 --> 00:31:18.590
and improve the
movement corridors for

00:31:18.591 --> 00:31:20.510
these species from
the yellow ones

00:31:20.511 --> 00:31:24.340
to the black ones that
are shown here, and help

00:31:24.341 --> 00:31:27.290
hopefully the long-term
persistence of the species.

00:31:27.291 --> 00:31:29.885
So, this was a brief overview,

00:31:29.886 --> 00:31:32.890
but this basically just gives you

00:31:32.891 --> 00:31:34.820
a sample of the kind
of methodologies

00:31:34.821 --> 00:31:37.095
that we use in the
conservation space again,

00:31:37.096 --> 00:31:39.405
leveraging machine
learning as well as

00:31:39.406 --> 00:31:43.175
optimization and game
theory to help these NGOs.

00:31:43.176 --> 00:31:45.770
So, thank you very much.

